::GEO_U04_L01_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Two figures have corresponding sides 3 and 6. What is the scale factor from the first figure to the second? {
=A. 2#The scale factor is <sup>6</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub> = 2.
~B. 3#This uses the first side length instead of the ratio.
~C. &frac12;#This is the scale factor from the second figure to the first.
~D. 9#This adds the side lengths instead of dividing.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which evidence supports figure similarity? {
~A. The figures are drawn close together.#Location does not prove similarity.
=B. Corresponding angles are congruent and corresponding sides are proportional.#This is the definition-level evidence for similarity.
~C. The figures have the same color.#Color is not a geometric property.
~D. Only one pair of sides is proportional.#One pair of sides is not enough to establish all side proportionality.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Side ratios are <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>, <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>6</sub>, and <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>10</sub>. What do these ratios show? {
~A. Only the first sides match.#All three ratios matter, not only the first.
~B. The figures are congruent only.#Congruence would require scale factor 1.
=C. The side lengths are proportional.#Each ratio simplifies to &frac12;, so the sides are proportional.
~D. The ratios are unequal.#The ratios are equal after simplifying.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A figure is enlarged with scale factor 3. What happens to corresponding side lengths? {
~A. They are divided by 3.#Dividing by 3 would describe the reverse comparison.
~B. They stay the same.#Side lengths stay the same only for rigid motions.
~C. They become 3 units total.#Scale factor is a multiplier, not a final total length.
=D. They are multiplied by 3.#A scale factor of 3 multiplies each corresponding length by 3.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement avoids relying only on appearance? {
=A. The figures are similar because all corresponding side ratios equal <sup>4</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>.#Equal corresponding side ratios are mathematical evidence for similarity.
~B. The figures are similar because they look alike.#Looking alike is not enough evidence.
~C. The figures are similar because both are blue.#Color is not geometric evidence.
~D. The figures are similar because one is nearby.#Location is not similarity evidence.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>If corresponding side lengths are 5 and 20, what is the scale factor from the first to the second? {
~A. 15#This subtracts instead of dividing.
=B. 4#<sup>20</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub> = 4.
~C. &frac14;#This is the reverse scale factor.
~D. 25#This adds the lengths.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which scale factor would reduce a figure to half its side lengths? {
~A. 2#A factor of 2 enlarges the figure.
~B. 1#A factor of 1 keeps the figure the same size.
=C. &frac12;#Multiplying by &frac12; makes each side half as long.
~D. 0#A scale factor of 0 collapses lengths and is not used for similar figures here.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Corresponding sides are 4 and 10, 6 and 15, 8 and 20. Are the figures similar by side ratios? {
~A. No, the side differences are not equal.#Similarity depends on ratios, not equal differences.
~B. Yes, because the side sums match.#Sums do not establish proportionality.
~C. No, because the figures are different sizes.#Similar figures can be different sizes.
=D. Yes, each ratio from first to second is 2.5.#The side ratios are equal: <sup>10</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> = <sup>15</sup>&frasl;<sub>6</sub> = <sup>20</sup>&frasl;<sub>8</sub> = 2.5.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>What does a scale factor of 1 mean? {
=A. The image is the same size as the original.#Multiplying by 1 keeps each length unchanged.
~B. The image is twice as large.#A factor of 2 doubles lengths.
~C. The image is half as large.#A factor of &frac12; halves lengths.
~D. The image has no side lengths.#A scale factor of 1 does not remove side lengths.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which pair of ratios is proportional? {
~A. &frac45; and &frac54;#These ratios are reciprocals, not equal.
=B. <sup>6</sup>&frasl;<sub>9</sub> and &frac23;#<sup>6</sup>&frasl;<sub>9</sub> simplifies to &frac23;, so the ratios are equal.
~C. &frac38; and <sup>6</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub>#&frac38; does not equal &frac12;.
~D. <sup>7</sup>&frasl;<sub>10</sub> and <sup>10</sup>&frasl;<sub>7</sub>#These ratios are reciprocals, not equal.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Two figures have corresponding sides 3 and 6. What is the scale factor from the first figure to the second? {
~A. 3#This uses the first side length instead of the ratio.
~B. &frac12;#This is the scale factor from the second figure to the first.
=C. 2#The scale factor is <sup>6</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub> = 2.
~D. 9#This adds the side lengths instead of dividing.
}

